Problem Understanding
Fine-tuning a small open-source language model to perform instruction following across Sanskrit $\leftrightarrow$ English tasks 
(translation, shloka explanation, and Q&A). Sanskrit presents domain-specific challenges, including morphologically dense compound 
structures (Samasa), euphonic fusion rules (Sandhi), and a low volume of digitally native instruction datasets compared to major 
modern languages.

Dataset Preparation
Source & Structure: Built an instruction-following dataset formatted in the Alpaca/ChatML schema featuring instruction, input, and 
output keys.
Task Mix: Included parallel Sanskrit–English translation pairs alongside verse context/philosophical explanations.
Formatting: Converted raw pairs into standard instruction blocks terminated with the model’s special End-Of-Sequence token (<|end_of_text|>).

Base Model Selection
Model: unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct.
Justification: Llama-3.2-1B offers an optimized transformer architecture featuring Grouped-Query Attention (GQA) and a large 128k 
context window. It exhibits lower base memory consumption than larger variants while maintaining pre-aligned instruction-following 
capabilities.

Fine-Tuning Approach
Methodology: QLoRA (Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation) via Hugging Face peft and Unsloth.
Quantization: 4-bit NormalFloat (NF4) base weight loading using bitsandbytes.
Adapters: Target modules configured across linear layers (q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj, gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj) with LoRA 
rank $r=16$ and scaling factor $\alpha=16$.
Optimization: Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFTTrainer) using adamw_8bit optimizer and a linear learning rate scheduler set to $2 \times 10^{-4}$.

Hardware Constraints & Optimizations
Environment: Google Colab running on a single T4 GPU (~15 GB VRAM).
VRAM Optimizations:
4-bit base model quantization to minimize memory footprint.
Unsloth gradient checkpointing to reduce activation memory usage during backward passes.\
Explicit GPU VRAM garbage collection (gc.collect() and torch.cuda.empty_cache()) between training and inference runs to avoid CUDA 
Out-Of-Memory (OOM) exceptions.

Evaluation Methodology
Qualitative Baseline Comparisons: Evaluated target output responses before and after applying LoRA adapters.
Generation Metrics: Monitored training loss convergence across 60–100 steps alongside visual inspection of translation fidelity on 
held-out Sanskrit prompts (e.g., Satyameva Jayate translation tests).

Failure Cases
Devanagari Subword Fragmentation: Out-of-vocabulary or complex Sanskrit conjuncts split into granular sub-character tokens, leading 
to longer token generation sequences and occasional word-ending typos.
Complex Sandhi Resolution: Unsplit compound Sanskrit words occasionally caused partial hallucination or overly literal translations.

Challenges Encountered
Memory Errors in Combined Pipelines: Sequentially running training and inference in the same Python process caused VRAM exhaustion 
without explicit memory flushing or subprocess execution.
Quantization Trainer Check Failure: Standard SFTTrainer threw runtime errors when receiving pure 4-bit weights until explicitly wrapped
via prepare_model_for_kbit_training() and get_peft_model().
Environment Mismatches on Cloud Deployment: CPU deployment on Streamlit Cloud initially failed due to Python 3.14 wheel incompatibilities 
and heavy GPU dependency imports (bitsandbytes/unsloth), requiring fallback to a CPU-compatible PyTorch/Transformers pipeline.

What Would Be Improved With More Time
Vocabulary Expansion: Adapt the tokenizer by adding high-frequency Devanagari subwords to reduce token sequence length fragmentation.
Dataset Scaling: Expand the corpus using larger parallel datasets (e.g., AI4Bharat IndicCorp, OPUS parallel corpora, or Bhagavad Gita 
verse alignment pairs).
Automated Quantitative Evaluation: Integrate automated cross-lingual metrics like BLEU or chrF+ across test splits.













